  "output_text": "You can validate that your top hypoxia‐regulated, zotatifin‐repressed genes are bona fide HIF1α targets in two complementary ways:\n\n1. Overlap with Published HIF1α–ChIP Targets  \n   – Compile the 108 “hypoxia ↑ & zotatifin ↓” genes and compare them to a curated HIF1α ChIP-seq target list (for example, ENCODE or ChEA HIF1A datasets). A highly significant overlap (hypergeometric p ≪ 0.001) confirms that many of your DEGs are directly bound by HIF1α in vivo.\n\n2. Promoter Motif Enrichment  \n   – Scan the 2 kb upstream regions of your 108 genes for the canonical Hypoxia Response Element (HRE) consensus (RCGTG). Enrichment of HREs relative to a background gene set (adjusted p < 0.01) provides orthogonal evidence that these genes are direct transcriptional targets of HIF1α.\n\nTogether, ChIP‐overlap and motif‐enrichment give you high confidence that your RNA-seq hits are directly regulated by HIF1α.\n\nMechanistic model for zotatifin’s action on HIF1α and tumor suppression:  \n• HIF1α mRNA harbors a highly structured, GC-rich 5′ UTR and G-quadruplex motifs that make its translation exquisitely dependent on the eIF4F helicase subunit eIF4A.  \n• Zotatifin binds to eIF4A and “locks” it onto polypurine motifs, preventing ATP‐dependent unwinding of secondary structure.  \n• Under hypoxia, cells upregulate HIF1α synthesis via eIF4A‐mediated scanning; zotatifin blocks this step, sharply reducing nascent HIF1α protein.  \n• Loss of HIF1α protein prevents nuclear translocation and downstream activation of survival (e.g., BNIP3), angiogenesis (e.g., VEGFA), and metabolic reprogramming genes.  \n• This collapse of the hypoxia‐adaptation program impairs tumor cell viability and vascularization in vivo, thereby reducing both primary growth and metastatic spread.",
